/* ===== College: theory, evidence and the hard arguments ===== */
U({id:'coa',track:'co',title:'Value and exploitation',scene:'books',blurb:"The engine room of Marx's economics, and the case against it.",lessons:[
{t:'The labor theory of value',sub:'Commodities, labor time and surplus',items:[
 ["Marx begins *Capital* with the **commodity**: something made to be sold. It has a **use-value** (what it is good for) and an **exchange-value** (what it trades for).",
  SORT("Use-value or exchange-value?",{"Use-value":["A coat keeps you warm","Bread feeds you","A hammer drives nails"],"Exchange-value":["A coat trades for 20 yards of linen","Bread sells for $4 a loaf"]},"The same object has both. Marx thought capitalism makes exchange-value the goal and use-value just the means.")],
 ["The **labor theory of value**, which Marx took from Adam Smith and David Ricardo and reworked, says a commodity's value is set by the **socially necessary labor time** needed to make it: the time an average producer needs with normal tools.",
  CASE("Most weavers make a yard of cloth in one hour on power looms. One weaver with an old hand loom takes four hours.",M("By Marx's theory, how much value does the hand weaver's yard have?",["One hour's worth","Four hours' worth","Two and a half hours' worth","No value at all"],"Value follows the socially necessary time, not the time an inefficient producer happens to take."))],
 ["Workers sell their **labor power** (their capacity to work) for a wage that covers what it costs to keep them and their families going. But a day of actual **labor** creates more value than that.",
  M("What is the difference between labor power and labor?",["Capacity to work versus the work actually done","Skilled, well-paid work versus unskilled, low-paid work","Factory work versus farm work","Paid work versus housework"],"The gap between what labor power costs and what labor produces is, for Marx, the secret of profit.")],
 ["Marx split the working day into **necessary labor** (the hours that reproduce the wage) and **surplus labor** (the rest). The ratio of surplus to necessary is the **rate of exploitation**.",
  CASE("In an 8-hour day, a worker creates value equal to her own wage in the first 3 hours.",M("What is the rate of exploitation (surplus labor divided by necessary labor)?",["About 167%","About 38%","About 60%","Exactly 100%"],"Five surplus hours over three necessary hours is 5/3, about 167 percent."))],
 ["Owners can raise surplus two ways. **Absolute surplus value** comes from a longer working day. **Relative surplus value** comes from making workers more productive, so the wage is 'earned back' in fewer hours.",
  SORT("Which kind of surplus value?",{"Absolute":["Lengthening the shift to 12 hours","Cutting the lunch break"],"Relative":["Installing faster machines","Reorganizing the assembly line","Cheaper food lowering the cost of living"]},"Laws limiting the working day pushed firms toward the second route: technology.")],
 ["Marx split capital into **constant capital** (machines and materials, which pass on their value) and **variable capital** (wages, the only part that creates new value).",
  PAIR("Match Marx's term to its meaning.",[["Constant capital","Machines and raw materials"],["Variable capital","Money spent on wages"],["Surplus value","New value beyond the wage"],["Rate of profit","Surplus over total capital advanced"]],"In symbols: rate of profit = s / (c + v).")],
 ["Marx also described **commodity fetishism**: in a market, relations between people (who worked for whom) appear as relations between things (prices).",
  EX("Explain in your own words how Marx thinks profit can exist even when every commodity sells at its value.","The worker sells labor power at its full value, the wage needed to live. But in a day's work the worker creates more value than that wage. The owner, having bought the labor power fairly, owns the whole product, including the extra. So nobody is cheated in exchange, yet surplus value still flows to the owner.")]
]},
{t:'The critics',sub:'Marginalism and the transformation problem',items:[
 ["In the 1870s the **marginal revolution** (Jevons, Menger, Walras) offered a rival theory: value comes from **marginal utility**, the satisfaction a buyer gets from one more unit. Scarcity and wants set prices, not labor hours.",
  M("According to marginalism, what determines a good's value?",["How much buyers want one more unit","The hours of socially necessary labor it contains","The weight of raw materials","The wage of the worker"],"Marginalism became the basis of mainstream economics.")],
 ["The classic puzzle is the **water and diamond paradox**: water is vital but cheap, diamonds are useless but dear. Marginalists say water is abundant, so one more cup is worth little.",
  CASE("A bottle of water sells for $1 in a city and for $50 to a hiker stranded in a desert. The labor in each bottle is the same.",M("Which theory explains the difference most directly?",["Marginal utility","The labor theory of value","Historical materialism","Commodity fetishism"],"Marxists reply that the labor theory is about long-run averages for goods that can be reproduced, not one-off situations.")),{g:"Fifty dollars for water. That hiker should unionize."}],
 ["The economist **Eugen von Böhm-Bawerk** attacked Marx in 1896. If only labor creates value, industries with many workers and few machines should earn higher profit rates. But competition evens profit rates out.",
  TF("Böhm-Bawerk argued that Marx's theory predicts equal profit rates across industries, which matches reality.",false,"He argued the opposite: the theory implies unequal profit rates, but real profit rates tend to equalize. This is the heart of the transformation problem.")],
 ["Marx's answer in Volume 3 was that values are 'transformed' into **prices of production**: surplus value is pooled and shared out in proportion to total capital. Whether his math works is the **transformation problem**.",
  M("What is the transformation problem about?",["Getting from labor values to market prices","Turning peasant farms into modern factories over time","Converting money into gold","Changing wages into profits"],"A century of debate followed. Some Marxists think it is solved, some think it does not matter, and critics think it sinks the theory.")],
 ["Critics add other points: owners are paid for **waiting** and **risk**; entrepreneurs create value by spotting opportunities; and skilled and unskilled labor are hard to add up in one unit.",
  PICK("Which are standard objections to the labor theory of value?",["Profit rates equalize across industries","Scarce goods with little labor sell high","Risk and waiting deserve a return"],["Workers are paid wages","Machines wear out over time"],"That workers are paid wages and machines wear out are parts of Marx's own account.")],
 ["Some later socialists dropped the labor theory. **John Roemer** argued you can define exploitation by unequal ownership alone: people are exploited if they would be better off withdrawing with their share of society's assets.",
  CASE("Two groups do the same work with the same skill. One group owns the land and tools. The other must rent them and ends up poorer, with no wage contract at all.",M("Whose account of exploitation fits this without any labor theory of value?",["Roemer's","Böhm-Bawerk's","Walras's","Ricardo's"],"For Roemer the root is unequal ownership of productive assets, not the wage relation itself."))],
 ["So the moral charge (owners gain from others' work because they own) can be separated from the technical theory (labor time determines value). Many modern socialists keep the first without the second.",
  EX("State the transformation problem in your own words and say why it matters.","Marx says only labor creates surplus value, so labor-heavy industries should have higher profit rates than machine-heavy ones. But competition makes profit rates roughly equal. Marx tried to show how values turn into prices that equalize profits, and critics say the math is inconsistent. It matters because if values cannot be linked to real prices, the labor theory may not explain how capitalism actually works.")]
]}]});

U({id:'cob',track:'co',title:'State, ideas and power',scene:'peaks',blurb:'Why do people accept the system they live in? Gramsci and after.',lessons:[
{t:'What is the state for?',sub:'Marxist theories of the state',items:[
 ["The Manifesto gives the bluntest Marxist view of the state.",
  SRC("The executive of the modern state is but a committee for managing the common affairs of the whole bourgeoisie.","Marx and Engels, The Communist Manifesto, 1848",M("What does this claim about governments under capitalism?",["They serve the owning class as a whole","They are neutral referees between all classes","They mainly serve the poor","They have no real power"],"Note 'common affairs of the whole': the state may overrule individual capitalists for the good of the system."))],
 ["Engels predicted that once classes disappear the state would have nothing to enforce and would **wither away**.",
  SRC("The state is not 'abolished'. It dies out.","Friedrich Engels, Anti-Dühring, 1878",TF("Engels is saying the workers' first task is to abolish the state by decree.",false,"That was the anarchist view. Engels argued the state would fade on its own once there were no classes to hold down."))],
 ["Lenin's *State and Revolution* (1917) said the existing state cannot be taken over. It must be **smashed** and replaced by a commune-style state of workers' councils, which would then wither away.",
  ORD("Put Lenin's sequence in order.",["Workers overthrow the old state","A workers' state suppresses the old ruling class","Classes disappear","The state withers away"],"In the actual Soviet Union the state grew to enormous size instead. Stalin said it had to get stronger before it could wither.")],
 ["Social democrats took another view: with universal suffrage the state becomes a **contested arena**. A workers' party can win office and use the state to tame capital.",
  SORT("Whose view of the state?",{"Lenin":["The old state must be smashed","Parliament is a talking shop"],"Social democrats":["Elections can redirect the state","Reforms add up over time","The state can serve majorities"]},"The Swedish and British labor parties bet on the second view.")],
 ["A middle position stresses **structural dependence**: even a left government needs private firms to invest, so it must keep 'business confidence' or face capital flight and job losses.",
  CASE("A left government is elected on a promise to tax wealth heavily. Within weeks money pours out of the country, the currency falls, and the government shelves the tax.",M("Which idea does this illustrate?",["Structural dependence on capital","The withering away of the state","Commodity fetishism","The vanguard party"],"Something like this happened to France's Socialist government in 1981 to 1983.")),{h:"Winning office and holding power turn out to be different things."}],
 ["In the 1970s **Ralph Miliband** stressed that state elites share backgrounds and interests with business. **Nicos Poulantzas** replied that the state serves capital because of its structural role, whoever staffs it.",
  PAIR("Match the thinker to the emphasis.",[["Miliband","Who the state officials are"],["Poulantzas","The state's structural role"],["Lenin","Smash and replace the state"],["Bernstein","Use parliament for gradual reform"]],"Miliband's view is called instrumentalist and Poulantzas's structuralist.")],
 ["Anarchists and many liberals share one criticism of all these Marxist views: they have little to say about how to restrain the *socialist* state's own power.",
  EX("In your own words, why might a government elected to challenge business end up protecting it?","Because the government depends on private firms for jobs, growth and tax income. If owners stop investing or move money abroad, the economy slumps and voters blame the government. So even leaders who want to challenge capital are pushed to keep business confident. That pressure works regardless of who the officials are.")]
]},
{t:'Hegemony',sub:'Gramsci on consent',items:[
 ["**Antonio Gramsci**, an Italian Communist jailed by Mussolini from 1926, asked why revolution had failed in the West. His answer: ruling classes rule by **consent** as well as force.",
  M("What question was Gramsci trying to answer?",["Why workers in the West did not revolt","How to plan a national economy without prices","Why profit rates tend to fall","How to win a guerrilla war"],"He wrote his answer in notebooks smuggled out of prison.")],
 ["**Hegemony** is leadership through consent: a ruling group makes its own outlook feel like plain common sense to everyone, through schools, churches, newspapers and everyday habits.",
  CASE("A factory worker says: 'There will always be bosses and workers. That's just how the world is. Anyone can get rich if they try hard enough.'",M("For Gramsci, what is this an example of?",["Hegemony working as 'common sense'","Direct rule by the force of police and army","A war of manoeuvre","Surplus value"],"The worker has not been forced. The arrangement simply seems natural, which is how hegemony works."))],
 ["Gramsci separated **political society** (the state's force: police, courts, army) from **civil society** (schools, media, unions, churches) where consent is organized.",
  SORT("Political society or civil society?",{"Political society":["The police","The courts","The army"],"Civil society":["Newspapers","Churches","Schools and universities"]},"In the West, Gramsci thought, civil society is a thick line of defense behind the state.")],
 ["So strategy must differ. In Russia a direct assault on the state, a **war of manoeuvre**, worked. In the West socialists needed a long **war of position**: building influence across civil society first.",
  PAIR("Match Gramsci's term to its meaning.",[["War of manoeuvre","A direct assault on state power"],["War of position","A long struggle within civil society"],["Hegemony","Rule through consent"],["Organic intellectual","A thinker rooted in a class"]],"He thought every class produces its own 'organic intellectuals,' from managers to shop stewards.")],
 ["Gramsci also wrote the best-known line about political crises.",
  SRC("The crisis consists precisely in the fact that the old is dying and the new cannot be born; in this interregnum a great variety of morbid symptoms appear.","Antonio Gramsci, Prison Notebooks, about 1930",M("What kind of moment is Gramsci describing?",["An in-between period of instability","A sudden, complete and final revolution","A long era of calm","The start of a new golden age"],"He was writing about the 1920s and 1930s, when the old liberal order had lost authority and fascism filled the gap."))],
 ["Gramsci's ideas spread far beyond Marxism. Movements of the right as well as the left now talk about winning 'the culture' before winning elections.",
  TF("Gramsci believed ruling classes hold power through force alone.",false,"His central claim is that consent, built in civil society, matters as much as force.")],
 ["Critics say hegemony can become an all-purpose excuse: if workers disagree with socialists, it must be 'false consciousness' rather than a reasoned choice.",
  EX("Explain hegemony in your own words, and give one way it can be criticized.","Hegemony is a ruling group's ability to make its view of the world seem like common sense, so people accept the system willingly rather than being forced. It works through schools, media, religion and daily habits. A criticism is that it can be used to dismiss what working people actually think: any disagreement with the theorist gets explained away as brainwashing.")]
]}]});

U({id:'coc',track:'co',title:'Revolution theorized',scene:'world',blurb:'Lenin, Luxemburg, Trotsky and Mao on how power is won.',lessons:[
{t:'Lenin and his critics',sub:'Party, imperialism and democracy',items:[
 ["In *What Is to Be Done?* (1902) Lenin argued that workers by themselves develop only **trade-union consciousness** (a fight for better terms). Socialist consciousness has to be brought in by a party of professional revolutionaries.",
  M("What did Lenin mean by 'trade-union consciousness'?",["Fighting for better pay within the system","A wish to abolish all unions and parties at once","Loyalty to the factory owner","A plan for workers' councils"],"His opponents the Mensheviks wanted a broad, open party instead.")],
 ["The party ran on **democratic centralism**: free debate before a decision, strict unity after it. In 1921 the Bolsheviks banned organized factions, and the 'centralism' swallowed the 'democratic.'",
  ORD("Put the steps of democratic centralism, as described on paper, in order.",["Members debate the question openly","A vote decides the line","All members carry out the decision","Open opposition to the decision stops"],"In practice, after 1921, the debate stage shrank toward nothing.")],
 ["The young Trotsky saw the danger in 1904.",
  SRC("The party organization substitutes itself for the party, the Central Committee substitutes itself for the organization, and finally a 'dictator' substitutes himself for the Central Committee.","Leon Trotsky, Our Political Tasks, 1904",M("What is Trotsky predicting about Lenin's kind of party?",["Power will narrow to one man","It will grow too large to manage","It will refuse to take power","It will merge with the unions"],"Trotsky later joined Lenin and helped build that state. Stalin then did exactly what the 1904 pamphlet predicted."))],
 ["In *Imperialism* (1916) Lenin argued capitalism had entered a stage of monopolies and **finance capital** that export capital and carve up the world, with war as the result.",
  PICK("Which features did Lenin assign to imperialism as a stage of capitalism?",["Dominance of monopolies","Export of capital abroad","Division of the world among great powers"],["Disappearance of banks","A return to small workshops"],"He also argued that profits from empire let capitalists 'buy off' a layer of better-paid workers at home.")],
 ["That let Lenin argue revolution could start at capitalism's **weakest link**, a backward country like Russia, instead of the most advanced one as Marx expected.",
  CASE("In 1917 Russia is mostly peasants, with a small working class concentrated in a few huge factories, and its state has collapsed in a losing war.",M("Which idea justified attempting a socialist revolution there?",["The weakest link","Marginal utility","Sectoral bargaining","The war of position"],"The Mensheviks said Russia was not ready and a workers' party seizing power there would have to rule by force. Lenin bet that revolution in Germany would come to the rescue. It did not."))],
 ["Luxemburg had criticized Lenin's centralism as early as 1904, and again in 1918 for dissolving the elected Constituent Assembly after the Bolsheviks lost the vote.",
  TF("The Bolsheviks won a majority in the 1917 Constituent Assembly election and then let it govern.",false,"They won about a quarter of the vote, behind the peasant-based Socialist Revolutionaries, and shut the Assembly down after one day in January 1918.")],
 ["So the central dispute is whether a minority party may seize and hold power on behalf of a class. Leninists say history demanded it. Critics say it built dictatorship in from the start.",
  EX("In your own words, set out the strongest criticism other socialists made of Lenin's vanguard party.","If a small disciplined party claims to know the workers' true interests better than the workers do, it can justify overruling them. Once it bans other parties and then its own internal factions, nothing checks its leaders. Power narrows from the class to the party, to the committee, to one person, which is what happened under Stalin.")]
]},
{t:'Trotsky and Mao',sub:'Two heirs, two directions',items:[
 ["Trotsky's **permanent revolution**: in a backward country the weak capitalist class cannot lead a democratic revolution, so workers must, and they will be driven on to socialist measures. But the revolution survives only if it spreads abroad.",
  M("Why did Trotsky think a revolution in Russia had to spread?",["A backward country could not build socialism alone","Russia had too many factories to manage without help","Lenin had ordered it in 1902","Other countries had asked to join"],"Stalin's answer was 'socialism in one country.'")],
 ["In exile Trotsky called the USSR a **degenerated workers' state**: property was still nationalized, but a bureaucratic caste had taken political power from the workers.",
  SORT("Trotsky or Stalin?",{"Trotsky":["Permanent revolution","The bureaucracy betrayed the revolution"],"Stalin":["Socialism in one country","Rapid forced collectivization","Show trials of old Bolsheviks"]},"Critics of both note that Trotsky had himself crushed Kronstadt and backed the ban on factions.")],
 ["Mao adapted Marxism to a peasant country. Revolution would come from the countryside surrounding the cities through **protracted people's war**.",
  SRC("A revolution is not a dinner party, or writing an essay, or painting a picture, or doing embroidery.","Mao Zedong, Report on the Peasant Movement in Hunan, 1927",M("What is Mao's point?",["Revolution is violent and cannot be gentle","Revolution should always be careful and polite","Artists make the best revolutionaries","Peasants should avoid politics"],"He goes on to call revolution 'an act of violence by which one class overthrows another.'"))],
 ["Mao's **mass line** said the party should gather the scattered ideas of the people, turn them into policy and take them back. In practice the party decided what the masses 'really' wanted.",
  CASE("Party officials tour villages, hear that peasants want more land and lower rents, write this into a program, and return to organize the villages around it.",M("Which Maoist idea is this?",["The mass line","Permanent revolution","Democratic centralism","Socialism in one country"],"'From the masses, to the masses' was the slogan."))],
 ["Mao also argued class struggle continues *after* the revolution, even inside the Communist Party. That idea launched the **Cultural Revolution** in 1966, which killed somewhere between several hundred thousand and two million people.",
  ORD("Put these events in Mao's China in order.",["Land reform","The Great Leap Forward","The Cultural Revolution","Deng's market reforms"],"Early 1950s, 1958 to 1962, 1966 to 1976, and from 1978.")],
 ["Maoism inspired movements from Peru to Nepal to the U.S. Black Panther Party. Its record includes the Khmer Rouge in Cambodia, who killed roughly a quarter of the population between 1975 and 1979.",
  PAIR("Match the concept to the thinker.",[["Mass line","Mao"],["Degenerated workers' state","Trotsky"],["Weakest link","Lenin"],["Spontaneity of the masses","Luxemburg"]],"Luxemburg trusted workers' own mass strikes more than party direction.")],
 ["All four thinkers wrestled with the same gap: Marx expected revolution where capitalism was most developed, and it kept happening where capitalism was least developed.",
  EX("Why is it a problem for Marx's theory that revolutions succeeded in Russia and China instead of Britain or Germany?","Marx thought socialism would grow out of advanced capitalism, which creates both great wealth and a large working class. Russia and China were poor and mostly peasant. So revolutionaries there had to build industry themselves, by state force, and the working class was too small to rule directly. Critics say this explains why those states became dictatorships. It also suggests Marx's theory of history did not predict what happened.")]
]}]});

U({id:'cod',track:'co',title:'No bosses, or no planners?',scene:'coop',blurb:'Anarchism, market socialism and the fight over whether planning can work.',lessons:[
{t:'Anarchism',sub:'Socialism without the state',items:[
 ["**Pierre-Joseph Proudhon** was the first to call himself an anarchist. His slogan 'property is theft' targeted income from owning without working: rent, interest, profit. He defended small 'possession' by those who use it.",
  M("What did Proudhon mean by 'property is theft'?",["Income from mere ownership is unearned","All personal belongings should be banned","Thieves should not be punished","The state should own everything"],"His system, **mutualism**, pictured self-employed workers and co-ops trading through a people's bank offering free credit.")],
 ["Anarchism then divided. **Bakunin's collectivists** wanted workers' associations to own the means of production. **Kropotkin's anarcho-communists** wanted free distribution by need, with no wages at all.",
  PAIR("Match the anarchist current to its core idea.",[["Mutualism","Co-ops and free credit"],["Collectivism","Workers' associations own industry"],["Anarcho-communism","Free distribution by need"],["Anarcho-syndicalism","Revolutionary unions run the economy"]],"The IWW was strongly influenced by syndicalism.")],
 ["Kropotkin, a trained geographer, wrote *Mutual Aid* (1902), arguing that cooperation within species is as important in evolution as competition.",
  TF("Kropotkin argued that evolution proves life is nothing but a war of each against all.",false,"He argued the reverse: that mutual aid is a major factor in survival, among animals and in human history.")],
 ["Anarchism's largest experiment came in the **Spanish Civil War**. In 1936, in Catalonia and Aragon, the anarcho-syndicalist union CNT led workers and peasants to collectivize factories, transit and farmland.",
  CASE("In Barcelona in late 1936 the trams are run by their workers' union, restaurants have no tipping, and a visiting English writer notes that 'waiters and shop-walkers looked you in the face and treated you as an equal.'",M("Who was the writer?",["George Orwell","John Maynard Keynes","Friedrich Hayek","Ernest Hemingway"],"Orwell fought there and described it in *Homage to Catalonia*. He also described how the Soviet-backed Communists suppressed the anarchists and independent Marxists in 1937.")),{g:"No tipping. As a seagull I have mixed feelings."}],
 ["The collectives were crushed twice over: by Communist-led government forces in 1937 and by Franco's victory in 1939.",
  ORD("Put these events in Spain in order.",["Military uprising against the Republic","Workers collectivize industry in Catalonia","Street fighting between Communists and anarchists in Barcelona","Franco wins the war"],"July 1936, summer and autumn 1936, May 1937, and 1939.")],
 ["Standard objections to anarchism: how would a stateless society defend itself, settle disputes, or coordinate millions of people? Anarchists point to federations of councils. Critics say that is a state by another name, or too weak to survive.",
  SORT("Anarchist claim, or objection to anarchism?",{"Anarchist claim":["Any state breeds a ruling elite","Free federation can coordinate society"],"Objection":["No way to defend against armies","Disputes need a final authority","Large economies need coordination"]},"The debate is unresolved, since no anarchist society has lasted long enough to test it.")],
 ["Anarchists were the first socialists to predict that a Marxist state would become a new tyranny. That prediction is their strongest claim to a hearing.",
  EX("In your own words, what do anarchists and Marxists agree on and where do they part?","They agree on the goal: a classless society without exploitation, and eventually without a state. They part on the route. Marxists after Marx held that workers must first capture state power and use it. Anarchists argue that any state, including a workers' state, creates a new ruling group, so the state must be dismantled at once and society organized from below.")]
]},
{t:'The calculation debate',sub:'Mises, Hayek, Lange',items:[
 ["In 1920 the Austrian economist **Ludwig von Mises** argued rational socialism is *impossible*. Without private ownership of capital goods there is no market for them, so no real prices, so no way to compare the costs of different ways of producing.",
  SRC("Where there is no free market, there is no pricing mechanism; without a pricing mechanism, there is no economic calculation.","Ludwig von Mises, Economic Calculation in the Socialist Commonwealth, 1920",M("What does Mises say planners would lack?",["Prices to compare costs","Workers willing to work","Enough raw materials","Good intentions"],"His claim is not that planners are lazy or corrupt. It is that they would be calculating in the dark."))],
 ["The Polish socialist **Oskar Lange** replied in the 1930s. A planning board could set trial prices, tell managers to minimize costs at those prices, then raise prices of goods in shortage and lower those in surplus.",
  ORD("Put Lange's trial-and-error procedure in order.",["The planning board announces prices","Managers choose outputs that minimize cost","Shortages and surpluses appear","The board adjusts prices and repeats"],"It imitates what a market does, with public ownership. This is the classic model of market socialism.")],
 ["**Friedrich Hayek** deepened the critique in 1945. The real problem is **knowledge**: the facts planners need are local, changing and often unspoken, held by millions of people.",
  SRC("The knowledge of the circumstances of which we must make use never exists in concentrated or integrated form but solely as the dispersed bits of incomplete and frequently contradictory knowledge which all the separate individuals possess.","F. A. Hayek, The Use of Knowledge in Society, 1945",M("What is Hayek's central point?",["No central mind can gather what is known","Planners are usually dishonest and self-serving","Workers know less than managers","Markets always reach perfect outcomes"],"For Hayek prices are a communication system that lets people act on knowledge nobody holds in one place."))],
 ["Hayek added that market competition is a **discovery procedure**. Lange's managers have no reason to invent new products or risk new methods, and no one to go bankrupt.",
  CASE("A state shoe factory fulfills its plan every year. It has made the same three styles since 1960. No rival exists, and its managers are judged only on meeting the target.",M("Which critique of planning does this best illustrate?",["Hayek's point about innovation and discovery","The transformation problem","Lenin's theory of imperialism","Gramsci's war of position"],"Soviet-type economies were notably weak at consumer innovation."))],
 ["The Hungarian economist **János Kornai** named another flaw: the **soft budget constraint**. State firms that lose money get bailed out, so they have little reason to economize, and they hoard inputs, creating chronic shortage.",
  M("What is a soft budget constraint?",["Loss-making firms expect to be rescued","Workers may set their own pay without limit","Prices change every single day","Banks refuse to lend to anyone"],"Kornai noted it is not unique to socialism. Big banks bailed out in 2008 had soft budget constraints too.")],
 ["Socialists have several replies. Modern computing might solve the calculation side. Giant firms like Walmart already plan internally at huge scale. And market socialism keeps prices while changing ownership.",
  SORT("Is this a reply to Mises and Hayek, or a point in their favor?",{"Socialist reply":["Big corporations plan internally","Worker co-ops can use market prices","Computers can process vast data"],"Point for Mises and Hayek":["Soviet shops had chronic shortages","Planned economies lagged in innovation"]},"Hayekians answer that computers handle data, but the knowledge problem is about facts never written down in the first place.")],
 ["Most economists think Mises and Hayek won the argument against *comprehensive central planning*. Whether they refuted *all* socialism, including market socialism, is still disputed.",
  EX("In your own words, what is the difference between Mises's objection and Hayek's?","Mises said that without private ownership there are no real market prices for machines and materials, so planners cannot calculate which production method costs least. Hayek's point is broader: the knowledge an economy needs is scattered among millions of people, much of it local and unspoken, and no central body could ever collect it. Prices and competition are how that knowledge gets used and how new knowledge is discovered.")]
]}]});

U({id:'coe',track:'co',title:'The Soviet economy up close',scene:'mill',blurb:'How planning actually ran, what it built and why it stalled.',lessons:[
{t:'Building the plan',sub:'1928 to 1953',items:[
 ["The 1920s **industrialization debate** set **Bukharin**, who wanted to keep NEP and let peasants prosper so industry could grow gradually, against **Preobrazhensky**, who argued for squeezing the peasantry to fund fast industrial growth.",
  SORT("Bukharin or Preobrazhensky?",{"Bukharin":["Keep the New Economic Policy","Let peasants enrich themselves"],"Preobrazhensky":["Extract a surplus from agriculture","Prioritize heavy industry fast","'Primitive socialist accumulation'"]},"Stalin first sided with Bukharin, then adopted an extreme form of the other program and had both men shot.")],
 ["Collectivization (from 1929) was meant to secure grain for cities and exports. Peasants slaughtered livestock rather than hand it over. The state kept taking grain, and in 1932 to 1933 between about 5 and 7 million people starved, in Ukraine, Kazakhstan and southern Russia.",
  M("Roughly how many people died in the Soviet famine of 1932 to 1933?",["About 5 to 7 million","About 50,000","About 500,000","About 40 million"],"Kazakhstan lost more than a third of its ethnic Kazakh population. Whether the Ukrainian famine was genocide is debated by historians. Many governments recognize it as one.")],
 ["Planning worked through **material balances**: for each key good, planners matched sources (output, imports, stocks) against uses (inputs to other industries, consumption, exports) in physical units.",
  CASE("Planners want 10 percent more tractors. That needs more steel, which needs more coal, which needs more mining machines, which need more steel.",M("What difficulty of material-balance planning does this show?",["Every target ripples through all the others","Tractors were unpopular with collective farmers","Coal was priced too high","Steel could not be measured"],"With thousands of goods, planners could never make it fully consistent. They prioritized heavy industry and let consumer goods absorb the errors."))],
 ["Managers were judged on **gross output**. They responded by hiding capacity to get easy targets, hoarding materials and workers, and rushing production at month's end ('**storming**').",
  PICK("Which behaviors did plan targets encourage in Soviet managers?",["Hoarding spare materials","Understating what the plant could do","Rushing output at the deadline"],["Cutting prices to win customers","Advertising heavily"],"Planners knew this and responded with the 'ratchet': this year's result became next year's minimum, which made managers even more careful not to overfulfill.")],
 ["It did build industry. On Western estimates Soviet output grew fast from 1928 to about 1970. The economist Robert Allen argues living standards of survivors rose substantially. Others reply the same growth was possible without the terror.",
  TF("Western economists agree the Soviet economy never grew at all.",false,"Most estimates show rapid growth into the 1960s, driven by moving labor and capital into industry. The dispute is about its cost and whether it could last.")],
 ["This is **extensive growth**: adding more inputs (workers, machines, land). **Intensive growth** means getting more from each input through better technology and organization.",
  SORT("Extensive or intensive growth?",{"Extensive":["Moving peasants into factories","Building more steel mills","Opening new farmland"],"Intensive":["A new process that halves energy use","Better management raising output per worker"]},"The Soviet system was good at the first and poor at the second.")],
 ["Once the pool of unused labor and resources ran dry, extensive growth had to slow.",
  EX("Why did the Soviet model grow fast at first and then slow down? Use the idea of extensive and intensive growth.","Early growth came from adding inputs: moving millions of peasants into factories, building plants and opening mines. That is extensive growth, and central planning could command it. But inputs run out. Further growth needed higher productivity through innovation, which is intensive growth. Planning gave managers no reason to innovate or take risks, so growth slowed from the 1960s on.")]
]},
{t:'Stagnation and the end',sub:'1953 to 1991',items:[
 ["After Stalin, terror ended and living standards rose. Khrushchev built mass housing and promised to overtake America. Reforms in 1965 under **Kosygin** tried to give managers profit targets, then were quietly rolled back.",
  ORD("Put these Soviet leaders in order.",["Stalin","Khrushchev","Brezhnev","Gorbachev"],"Stalin died in 1953, Khrushchev was removed in 1964, Brezhnev died in 1982, and Gorbachev led from 1985 to 1991.")],
 ["Under Brezhnev came a tacit **social contract**: secure jobs, low fixed prices and little work pressure in exchange for political silence. The joke was: 'They pretend to pay us and we pretend to work.'",
  CASE("A Soviet worker in 1978 cannot be fired, pays almost nothing for rent and bread, and spends hours each week queueing or trading favors to get meat and shoes.",PICK("Which features of the Brezhnev-era economy does this show?",["Job security","Subsidized basic prices","Chronic shortages"],["Mass unemployment","Rapid price inflation in shops"],"Inflation was hidden: prices stayed fixed while goods disappeared, so it showed up as queues and black markets.")),{g:"They pretend to pay us and we pretend to work. Finally, a labor theory I understand."}],
 ["Because goods were scarce at official prices, a huge **second economy** grew: bribes, moonlighting, goods 'falling off the truck.' Getting things depended on **blat**, personal connections.",
  M("What was 'blat' in Soviet life?",["Using personal connections to get scarce goods","A five-year production target for heavy industry","A medal for model workers","The state planning agency"],"It was how the shortage economy actually functioned day to day.")],
 ["Growth slid toward zero by the early 1980s. Oil exports paid for grain imports, so the 1986 oil price collapse hit hard. Military spending took perhaps 15 percent or more of output.",
  PICK("Which factors do historians cite for the late Soviet economic crisis?",["Falling oil income","A heavy military burden","Weak productivity growth"],["Too many private businesses","Excessive consumer advertising"],"Scholars disagree about how much each mattered, and whether the system was doomed or was broken by the way it was reformed.")],
 ["Gorbachev's **perestroika** loosened central control over firms without creating real markets or hard budgets. Firms raised wages, the state printed money, and shortages got much worse.",
  CASE("In 1988 enterprises are freed to set wages but prices stay fixed by the state and loss-making firms still get bailed out.",M("What would you expect to happen?",["Shortages worsen as wages outrun goods","Shops overflow with unsold goods nobody wants","Prices in state shops fall","Firms go bankrupt in large numbers"],"More money chasing the same goods at fixed prices means emptier shelves. This is what happened."))],
 ["After 1991 Russia tried **shock therapy**: rapid price liberalization and privatization. Output fell by roughly 40 percent, male life expectancy dropped by about six years, and a few 'oligarchs' acquired the main assets.",
  TF("Russia's economy grew strongly in the first five years after the Soviet collapse.",false,"It shrank dramatically. China's gradual, state-led marketization from 1978 is the usual contrast, though China started from a very different, mostly rural economy.")],
 ["So the Soviet story does not settle the wider argument. It shows comprehensive central planning under one-party rule failing. It also shows a sudden leap to markets can be catastrophic.",
  EX("In your own words, why did Gorbachev's economic reforms make things worse before the collapse?","Perestroika took away the planners' control over firms without putting market discipline in its place. Firms could raise wages and were still rescued if they lost money, while prices stayed fixed. So people had more rubles but there were no more goods, and shortages deepened. The old system stopped working before a new one existed.")]
]}]});

U({id:'cof',track:'co',title:'Revolution in the Global South',scene:'world',blurb:'China, Cuba, Chile and the socialisms of decolonization.',lessons:[
{t:'China from Mao to markets',sub:'1949 to today',items:[
 ["The early People's Republic carried out **land reform**: land was taken from landlords and given to peasant households. Perhaps one to two million landlords were killed in the process.",
  M("What did China's land reform of about 1950 do?",["Gave landlords' land to peasant families","Created giant state-owned farms at once","Sold farmland to foreign firms","Abolished farming in favor of industry"],"Collectivization came later, in the mid-1950s.")],
 ["The **Great Leap Forward** (1958) merged peasants into huge **people's communes**, set fantasy grain and steel targets, and punished officials who reported bad news. Inflated harvest reports led the state to take grain that did not exist.",
  ORD("Put the chain of the Great Leap famine in order.",["Officials report inflated harvests","The state requisitions grain based on those reports","Villages are left without food","Tens of millions die"],"Leaders who raised the alarm, like Peng Dehuai in 1959, were purged.")],
 ["The economist **Amartya Sen** drew a general lesson from comparing China with democratic India, which was poorer but had no comparable famine after independence.",
  SRC("No famine has ever taken place in the history of the world in a functioning democracy.","Amartya Sen, Development as Freedom, 1999",M("What mechanism does Sen have in mind?",["A free press and elections force rulers to respond","Democracies always manage to grow more food per person","Democracies have fewer people to feed","Dictatorships cannot import grain"],"Sen also noted that India's everyday malnutrition killed more people over decades than China's did, so each system failed in a different way."))],
 ["Under Mao, health and schooling still improved dramatically through 'barefoot doctors' and mass literacy drives.",
  DATA("Life expectancy at birth in China",[["1950",41,"about 41"],["1965",50,"about 50"],["1980",64,"about 64"]],M("What does the chart show?",["A large rise over thirty years","A steady fall under Mao","No change before 1980","A rise only after market reforms"],"The rise came before the market reforms. The famine years are a deep dip hidden between these points."),"Source: UN Population Division estimates")],
 ["Deng's reforms began in the countryside. The **household responsibility system** let families farm their own plots and sell what they grew above a quota. Farm output jumped.",
  CASE("In 1978 farmers in a Chinese village secretly agree to split communal land into family plots, keeping whatever each family grows above the state quota. The next harvest is far larger.",M("What general lesson did Chinese reformers take from cases like this?",["Incentives for the producer raise output","Communes should be made larger","Quotas should be doubled","Private plots should be banned"],"The village was Xiaogang in Anhui. The practice was legalized and spread nationwide."))],
 ["Today China has billionaires and stock exchanges alongside giant state-owned banks and enterprises and firm party control. The party calls it 'socialism with Chinese characteristics.' Many outside observers call it **state capitalism**.",
  SORT("Sort these features of China's economy today.",{"Market features":["Private companies","Stock exchanges","Billionaires"],"State control":["State-owned banks","Party committees inside firms","Five-year plans"]},"Whether to call the mix socialist is one of the most argued questions on the left.")],
 ["China's growth since 1978 has lifted roughly 800 million people out of extreme poverty by World Bank measures. It also has no independent unions, tight censorship and very high inequality.",
  EX("Why do people disagree about whether today's China is socialist?","Those who say yes point to Communist Party rule, state ownership of banks, land and key industries, and long-term planning. Those who say no point to private firms, wage labor, billionaires, high inequality and the ban on independent unions, all of which look like capitalism. The answer depends on whether socialism is defined by who holds state power or by how workers actually live and work.")]
]},
{t:'Cuba, Chile and African socialism',sub:'Armed, elected and homegrown roads',items:[
 ["Cuba's 1959 revolution nationalized U.S.-owned sugar mills, banks and utilities. The U.S. imposed an **embargo** (from 1960 to 1962) that still stands, and backed the failed **Bay of Pigs** invasion in 1961.",
  ORD("Put these events in order.",["Castro's rebels take Havana","The Bay of Pigs invasion fails","The Cuban Missile Crisis","The Soviet Union collapses and aid ends"],"1959, 1961, 1962 and 1991.")],
 ["Cuba's health and education numbers match those of rich countries, a point even critics concede. The state also jails dissidents, controls the press and allows one party. And many thousands have left by sea.",
  DATA("Infant deaths per 1,000 births, about 2019",[["Cuba",4,"about 4"],["United States",6,"about 6"],["Latin America average",14,"about 14"]],M("What does the chart show about Cuba?",["Its rate is low, close to the U.S.","Its rate is the highest in the whole region","It has no health system","Its rate is triple the U.S. rate"],"Some researchers question the official figure, noting pressure on doctors to meet targets. Even adjusted estimates are well below the regional average."),"Source: World Bank / UN estimates")],
 ["When Soviet subsidies ended in 1991, Cuba's economy shrank by about a third. Cubans call it the **Special Period**. The state opened to tourism and small private business to survive.",
  M("What caused Cuba's 'Special Period' in the 1990s?",["The loss of Soviet aid and trade","A sudden rise in sugar prices","The end of the U.S. embargo","A wave of new immigration"],"How much of Cuba's poverty is due to the embargo and how much to its own planning is a standing argument.")],
 ["In **Chile**, **Salvador Allende**, a Marxist, won the presidency in a free election in 1970. He nationalized copper (with a unanimous vote in Congress), raised wages and expanded land reform.",
  CASE("An elected socialist president nationalizes the main export industry and raises wages. Inflation soars, truck owners strike, the U.S. cuts off credit and funds his opponents, and the army overthrows him.",M("Which country and year is this?",["Chile, 1973","Cuba, 1959","Spain, 1936","Russia, 1917"],"General Pinochet's dictatorship followed, killing or 'disappearing' more than 3,000 people.")),{h:"Chile is why 'the democratic road' is still argued about."}],
 ["People draw opposite lessons. Some conclude that capital and the military will not allow an elected transition, so compromise is fatal. Others conclude Allende moved too fast with too narrow a majority (he won with 36 percent).",
  SORT("Which lesson from Chile?",{"'The democratic road is blocked'":["Elites will use force to keep property","Foreign powers will sabotage it"],"'Go slower, build a majority'":["He lacked majority support","Runaway inflation eroded backing","A broader coalition was needed"]},"Both readings draw on real facts about 1970 to 1973.")],
 ["In Africa, leaders argued socialism fit older communal traditions. **Kwame Nkrumah** (Ghana) pushed state-led industry. **Nyerere** (Tanzania) pushed village **ujamaa**. **Thomas Sankara** (Burkina Faso, 1983 to 1987) ran mass vaccination and tree-planting drives before being killed in a coup.",
  PAIR("Match the leader to what they are known for.",[["Nkrumah","Pan-Africanism and state-led industry in Ghana"],["Nyerere","Ujamaa villages in Tanzania"],["Sankara","Vaccination drives in Burkina Faso"],["Allende","An elected Marxist government in Chile"]],"Nkrumah's slogan was 'Seek ye first the political kingdom.'")],
 ["Outcomes were mixed at best. Many African socialist states ended up with one-party rule, inefficient state firms and debt crises in the 1980s, in a world economy stacked against commodity exporters.",
  EX("Give the two opposite lessons people draw from the overthrow of Allende in Chile.","One lesson: when an elected socialist government threatens property, the wealthy, the military and foreign powers will destroy it by sabotage and force, so the democratic road is a trap unless the left is ready for that. The other lesson: Allende had only about a third of the vote and moved fast into deep economic trouble, so the left needs a much broader majority and a slower pace before making radical changes.")]
]}]});

U({id:'cog',track:'co',title:'Labor, race and the American left',scene:'usa',blurb:'Where class and race met, clashed and sometimes joined.',lessons:[
{t:'Solidarity and its limits',sub:'1880s to 1940s',items:[
 ["Many early unions excluded Black, Asian and Mexican workers, and employers used excluded workers as strikebreakers. **W. E. B. Du Bois** argued white workers accepted low pay in exchange for a 'public and psychological wage' of racial status.",
  M("What did Du Bois mean by the 'psychological wage' of whiteness?",["Status that made up for low pay","A yearly bonus paid by employers","A tax on white workers","Free schooling for workers"],"The idea, from *Black Reconstruction* (1935), is central to debates about why American workers did not unite as a class.")],
 ["Du Bois also reframed the Civil War: enslaved people fleeing plantations and joining Union lines amounted to a **general strike** that helped decide the war.",
  TF("Du Bois described enslaved people's wartime flight from plantations as a kind of general strike.",true,"It made Black workers the active force in their own emancipation, not just recipients of it.")],
 ["Some unions organized across the color line from the start: the Knights of Labor in the 1880s, the IWW, and the Communist-led unions of the 1930s. The **CIO** made interracial organizing a principle in steel, auto and meatpacking.",
  SORT("Sort by organizing approach.",{"Organized by craft, often whites only":["Most AFL craft unions around 1900","Railroad brotherhoods"],"Organized whole industries across race":["The IWW","CIO packinghouse workers","The Knights of Labor at their peak"]},"The CIO's record was uneven, but it brought hundreds of thousands of Black workers into unions.")],
 ["The **Communist Party** in the 1930s was one of the few mostly white organizations to make anti-racism central: defending the Scottsboro defendants, organizing sharecroppers in Alabama, running Black candidates.",
  CASE("In 1931 nine Black teenagers in Alabama are sentenced to death on a false rape charge. A legal arm of one political party takes the case to the U.S. Supreme Court and organizes worldwide protests.",M("Which party's lawyers led the Scottsboro defense?",["The Communist Party","The Republican Party","The Democratic Party","The Prohibition Party"],"The NAACP was more cautious at first. The case won two landmark Supreme Court rulings on fair trials."))],
 ["The party's loyalty to Moscow repeatedly wrecked its credibility: it defended the Stalin purge trials and reversed its line overnight after the 1939 Nazi-Soviet pact.",
  PICK("Which facts damaged the U.S. Communist Party's standing?",["Defending Stalin's show trials","Switching positions after the 1939 pact","Taking direction from Moscow"],["Defending the Scottsboro defendants","Organizing industrial unions"],"Its civil rights and union work earned respect. Its subservience to Soviet foreign policy squandered it.")],
 ["**A. Philip Randolph** showed another model. In 1941 his threat of a mass march on Washington forced Roosevelt to ban discrimination in defense industries.",
  ORD("Put Randolph's career in order.",["Founds the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters","Wins a union contract from the Pullman Company","Threatens a 1941 march, winning a ban on defense-job bias","Leads the 1963 March on Washington"],"1925, 1937, 1941 and 1963.")],
 ["After the war, the South's 'Operation Dixie' organizing drive failed against segregation and red-baiting, and Taft-Hartley's anti-Communist oaths pushed the CIO to expel eleven left-led unions.",
  EX("In your own words, how did racism weaken the American labor movement?","Employers could divide workers by race, hiring excluded groups to break strikes, and many unions made it worse by barring Black, Asian and Mexican workers. White workers were often offered racial status in place of better pay. A divided working class could not build the kind of united unions and labor party that existed in Europe, and the unorganized, segregated South became a permanent low-wage zone.")]
]},
{t:'From the New Left to DSA',sub:'1960 to today',items:[
 ["**Michael Harrington**'s *The Other America* (1962) exposed mass poverty amid affluence and helped inspire the War on Poverty. Harrington, a socialist, later founded DSA and urged working as 'the left wing of the possible' inside the Democratic Party.",
  M("What strategy did Harrington argue for?",["Working within the Democratic Party","Forming an armed underground","Abstaining from all elections","Joining the Republican Party"],"Critics to his left called this giving up on independent politics.")],
 ["The 1963 march demanded jobs as well as rights. Randolph and Rustin followed with a **Freedom Budget** (1966): full employment, a guaranteed income and housing, about $185 billion over ten years. Congress ignored it as Vietnam spending grew.",
  SRC("Call it democracy, or call it democratic socialism, but there must be a better distribution of wealth within this country for all God's children.","Martin Luther King Jr., 1965",EX("King signed the foreword to the Freedom Budget. In your own words, why did he and its authors think civil rights laws alone were not enough?","Ending legal segregation gave Black Americans the right to sit at the lunch counter but not the money to buy the meal. Poverty, unemployment and bad housing would remain without jobs and income for everyone. They argued economic rights had to be added to civil rights, for poor people of every race."))],
 ["The **Black Panther Party** (founded in Oakland in 1966) mixed Marxism and Black nationalism, ran free breakfast programs and health clinics, and was targeted by the FBI's COINTELPRO campaign.",
  PICK("Which describe the Black Panther Party?",["Free breakfast programs for children","Influenced by Marxism and Maoism","Targeted by FBI covert operations"],["Founded by the AFL-CIO","Opposed to all community programs"],"FBI director J. Edgar Hoover called it the greatest threat to internal security. Police killed Chicago leader Fred Hampton in a 1969 raid.")],
 ["**Socialist feminists** argued that unpaid housework and care, done mostly by women, is what makes all paid work possible, yet it counts for nothing in wages or GDP.",
  CASE("A woman cooks, cleans and raises three children for twenty years with no wage. Her husband's employer benefits from a fed, clothed worker and a next generation of workers.",M("What do socialist feminists call this unpaid work?",["Reproductive labor","Surplus labor","Abstract labor","Constant capital"],"The 1970s 'Wages for Housework' campaign made the point by demanding pay for it."))],
 ["From the mid-1970s the postwar order unraveled: factories moved, unions shrank, and in 1981 President Reagan fired 11,000 striking air traffic controllers (**PATCO**), signaling open season on strikes.",
  DATA("Major strikes per year in the U.S. (1,000 workers or more)",[["1952",470],["1974",424],["1982",96],["2009",5]],M("What happened to large strikes after about 1980?",["They nearly disappeared","They doubled","They held steady","They became illegal"],"The number rose somewhat again after 2018 with teacher, auto and other strikes."),"Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics")],
 ["After the 2008 crash and Occupy Wall Street (2011), socialism re-entered public debate. Sanders's 2016 and 2020 campaigns, the election of DSA members such as Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, and new organizing at Amazon and Starbucks followed.",
  ORD("Put these in order.",["The financial crisis","Occupy Wall Street","Sanders's first presidential campaign","Zohran Mamdani elected mayor of New York"],"2008, 2011, 2016 and November 2025.")],
 ["A long-running argument divides the left: is the root problem **class**, with racism as a tool that divides workers, or are race and class separate systems that must each be confronted? Most scholars now see them as intertwined.",
  SORT("Which emphasis?",{"'Class first'":["Universal programs help everyone","Racism is used to split workers"],"'Race and class together'":["Universal programs can leave gaps","Black workers face both exploitation and exclusion","Movements must name racism directly"]},"Both sides usually agree on the programs. They differ on strategy and on how to talk about it.")]
]}]});

U({id:'coh',track:'co',title:"Social democracy's arc",scene:'city',blurb:'How the reformist road rose, peaked and ran into trouble.',lessons:[
{t:'The golden age',sub:'1945 to 1975',items:[
 ["After 1945 Western Europe built **welfare states** on a 'class compromise': labor accepted private ownership, and capital accepted unions, high taxes and full employment policy.",
  M("What did labor give up in the postwar class compromise?",["The demand to end private ownership","The right to vote","The right to form unions","Its welfare benefits"],"In West Germany the SPD formally dropped Marxism at Bad Godesberg in 1959.")],
 ["Britain's Labour government of 1945 created the **National Health Service** and nationalized coal, rail, steel and utilities, about a fifth of the economy.",
  PICK("What did the 1945 Labour government in Britain do?",["Created the National Health Service","Nationalized coal and railways","Expanded public housing"],["Abolished Parliament","Banned private shops"],"Critics on the left complained the nationalized industries were run by the same kind of managers as before, with no worker control.")],
 ["The economics came from **John Maynard Keynes**: governments can prevent mass unemployment by spending in slumps. Full employment in turn strengthens workers' bargaining power.",
  ORD("Put the Keynesian response to a slump in order.",["Private spending collapses","Government raises spending or cuts taxes","Demand for goods recovers","Firms rehire workers"],"Keynes was a liberal, not a socialist, and wanted to save capitalism from itself.")],
 ["Sweden's unions designed the **Rehn-Meidner model**: equal pay for equal work across firms ('solidarity wages'), so weak firms would fold and strong ones expand, with the state retraining displaced workers.",
  CASE("Under solidarity wages, a highly profitable Volvo plant pays the same wage as a struggling firm. Volvo's profits balloon because its wage bill is lower than it could afford.",M("What problem did this create that the Meidner Plan was meant to solve?",["Excess profits piling up with owners","Wages rising much too fast at Volvo","Unemployment among engineers","Too many strikes at weak firms"],"The wage-earner funds were designed to capture those 'excess profits' as collective worker ownership."))],
 ["The Polish economist **Michał Kalecki** had predicted in 1943 why business would eventually turn against permanent full employment.",
  SRC("'Discipline in the factories' and 'political stability' are more appreciated than profits by business leaders.","Michał Kalecki, Political Aspects of Full Employment, 1943",M("What is Kalecki's argument?",["Owners fear losing power over workers","Owners care only about profit","Workers dislike full employment","Governments cannot create jobs"],"If no one fears the sack, the boss's authority weakens. So business may prefer some unemployment even at the cost of lower profits."))],
 ["The results were real: from 1945 to 1975 Western economies grew fast, inequality fell and unemployment stayed low. In France these years are called 'the thirty glorious years.'",
  TF("Inequality in Western Europe and the U.S. rose steadily throughout 1945 to 1975.",false,"It fell or stayed low in this period and began rising again around 1980.")],
 ["Yet social democracy changed distribution far more than it changed ownership or control.",
  EX("What was the postwar 'class compromise,' in your own words?","Workers' parties and unions agreed to leave businesses in private hands and to keep the market economy. In return, owners accepted strong unions, high taxes, a welfare state and government policies aimed at full employment. Each side gave up its maximum demand in exchange for stability and shared growth.")]
]},
{t:'The retreat',sub:'1975 to today',items:[
 ["In the 1970s the model hit **stagflation**: high inflation and high unemployment together, which Keynesian theory said should not happen. Oil shocks, slowing productivity and a squeeze on profits all played a part.",
  M("What is stagflation?",["High inflation with high unemployment","Falling prices together with full employment","Rapid growth with stable prices","High taxes with low spending"],"It discredited Keynesian management and opened the door to a different doctrine.")],
 ["**Neoliberalism** (Thatcher from 1979, Reagan from 1981) answered with tight money, tax cuts, privatization, deregulation and attacks on union power.",
  SORT("Postwar social democracy or neoliberalism?",{"Postwar social democracy":["Nationalized industries","Full employment as the main goal","Strong legal support for unions"],"Neoliberalism":["Privatization","Low inflation as the main goal","Deregulated finance"]},"Thatcher defeated a year-long miners' strike in 1984 to 1985, a turning point for British labor.")],
 ["France tested the alternative. **François Mitterrand**'s Socialist government (1981) nationalized banks and major firms and raised wages. Capital fled, the franc was devalued three times, and in 1983 he reversed course.",
  ORD("Put Mitterrand's experience in order.",["Socialists win and nationalize banks","Capital flight and currency crises","The 'turn to austerity' of 1983","Privatizations under later governments"],"The lesson many drew was that 'Keynesianism in one country' could not work with open capital markets.")],
 ["By the 1990s center-left parties embraced the **Third Way** (Blair, Clinton, Schröder): accept markets and globalization, and add investment in education and modest redistribution.",
  CASE("A senior figure in a labor party says the party must drop its commitment to public ownership, be 'intensely relaxed about people getting filthy rich as long as they pay their taxes,' and win over business.",M("What is this approach called?",["The Third Way","The mass line","Syndicalism","Permanent revolution"],"Britain's Labour Party rewrote its Clause IV, which had called for common ownership, in 1995.")),{g:"Intensely relaxed about the filthy rich. I'm intensely relaxed about unattended sandwiches."}],
 ["Critics say the Third Way cost these parties their working-class base. Across Europe social democratic vote shares have fallen sharply since 2000, with voters moving to the populist right, greens and the radical left.",
  DATA("Vote share of the German SPD in federal elections",[["1998",40.9,"40.9%"],["2009",23.0,"23.0%"],["2025",16.4,"16.4%"]],M("What does the chart show?",["A long decline from the Third Way era","Steady growth since 1998","A collapse followed by full recovery","No change over time"],"The SPD's 2025 result was its worst in a national election since the 1880s."),"Source: German federal election results")],
 ["Explanations compete: globalization weakened unions; the industrial working class shrank; welfare states were already built; and parties became dominated by graduates. The economist **Thomas Piketty** calls the result a 'Brahmin left.'",
  PICK("Which explanations are offered for social democracy's decline?",["A shrinking industrial workforce","Mobile capital limiting national policy","Parties drifting toward educated voters"],["A ban on left parties","The end of elections in Europe"],"Each explanation points to a different remedy, which is why the argument is heated.")],
 ["Since 2008 some on the left have revived older ideas, including public ownership, sectoral bargaining and wealth taxes, arguing the retreat went too far.",
  EX("Why did Mitterrand's 1981 program fail, and what lesson did social democrats take from it?","His government raised wages and nationalized firms while France's neighbors were cutting back. Demand spilled into imports, investors moved money out and the currency came under attack. To stay in the European monetary system he reversed course in 1983. The lesson many drew was that one country cannot run an expansionary left program alone when capital moves freely, so parties moved toward accepting markets.")]
]}]});
